{"items":[{"tags":["node.js","react-native","ubuntu","npm","expo"],"question_score":4,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":72711934,"item_type":"question","score":4,"last_activity_date":1790833944,"creation_date":1655885863,"body":"I installed expo-cli in ubuntu using  and it installed with some warning.\n\nThen I tried to create a new project using  with blank template. But it failed and showing an error log given below\n\nDoes anyone know how to solve this?\n","excerpt":"Upgrade to chokidar 3 with 15x fewer dependencies\nnpm WARN deprecated querystring@0.2.0: The querystring API is considered Legacy. new code should use the <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> API instead.\nnpm WARN deprecated &hellip; ","title":"expo init showing Error in downloading and expracting due to TypeError"},{"tags":["javascript","reactjs","next.js"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":2,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79756559,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1788775003,"creation_date":1757061246,"body":"I made a search panel component, . On it, a button grabs the details from the form (Flight From, Flight To, Flight Date, and some other data), then sends that to a new page.\nHere&#39;s the :\n\nThat routes to another page, . This page now takes the parameters from the search panel, and uses those as parameters for an API call:\n\nThis works, until I add  at the very top, which I need for a useState to update a clicked  styling and store the ID.\nThen the page just repeatedly calls the fetch code, until I kill the project or navigate elsewhere.\nI have no idea what to do. Removing my code and simply adding something like a  would see  printed repeatedly in the console as well.\n","excerpt":"== &quot;&quot;)) {\n      const params = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>();\n\n      params.set(&#39;is_using_flight_number&#39;, isUsingFlightNum.toString());\n      params.set(&#39;flight_from&#39;, flightFrom);\n      params.set(&#39;flight_to&#39; &hellip; ","title":"Rerouting to new page with parameters and fetching from API results in API fetch loop"},{"tags":["reactjs","html5-history"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":3,"is_answered":false,"question_id":78385830,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1787855173,"creation_date":1714060466,"body":"In the react component below I want to display the current value of a url query param after updating it. Something like this is straightforward to do with react-router, but I am trying to implement manually in a more minimal app. The code below effectively updates the url, but does not trigger a re-render, so the displayed value never updates.\n\nAm I handling the navigation wrong? Does there need to be a  involved somehow? Is this functionality impossible without an outer component and/or some use of react state?\n","excerpt":"The code below effectively updates the url, but does not trigger a re-render, so the displayed value never updates.\nexport default function ExampleComponent() {\n    const params = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>(location.search &hellip; ","title":"how to trigger a react component re-render after changing query param (without react-router)"},{"tags":["typescript"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":2,"is_answered":false,"question_id":40991923,"item_type":"question","score":2,"last_activity_date":1786482080,"creation_date":1481016606,"body":"I&#39;ve just installed a self-made npm library on my project. \n\nnpm is able to generate every artifacts and I&#39;ve published them. So, now I&#39;m able to use the services I&#39;ve made: .\n\nThe package structure is:\n\n\n\nThe content of  is:\n\n\n\nThe content of  is:\n\n\n\nAnd the content of  is a regular typescript content:\n\n\n\nI&#39;m getting the next message:\n\n\n\nThis is the &#39;s content:\n\n\n\nAny ideas?\n","excerpt":".\n */\n\nimport { Inject, Injectable, Optional }                      from &#39;@angular/core&#39;;\nimport { Http, Headers, <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> }                    from &#39;@angular/http&#39;;\nimport { RequestMethod, RequestOptions &hellip; : any): Observable&lt;Response&gt; {\n        const path = this.basePath + `/users`;\n\n        let queryParameters = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>();\n        let headers = new Headers(this.defaultHeaders.toJSON()); // https &hellip; ","title":"*.ts is not a module"},{"tags":["javascript","local-storage","cross-domain"],"question_score":57,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79990880,"is_answered":false,"question_id":33957477,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1785990931,"creation_date":1785990931,"body":"The URL&#39;s search params on this website are accessible and modifiable.When reading and writing the URL&#39;s search params, as long as a unique key is used, it will not affect the website&#39;s functionality.Moreover,URL&#39;s search params supports cross-domain read/write.Therefore, it can serve as a small amount of cross-domain storage.The advantage is that there are no other expenses.And it&#39;s very simple.\n1.URL&#39;s search params reading method.\n\n2.URL&#39;s search params writing method.\n\n3.Can refer this answer Modifying a query string without reloading the page.Setting the URL&#39;s search params will not refresh the page.\n","excerpt":"foo=1&amp;bar=2&quot;);\nlet params = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>(url.search);\n\n// Add a third parameter.\nparams.set(&quot;baz&quot;, 3);\nparams.toString(); // &quot;foo=1&amp;bar=2&amp;baz=3&quot;\n\n3.Can refer this answer Modifying a query string &hellip; ","title":"cross domain localstorage with javascript"},{"tags":["javascript","typescript","amazon-web-services","amazon-cloudfront"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79983928,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79983514,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1784740509,"creation_date":1784740509,"body":"This issue is . That creates a query string and encodes the leading  so cloudfront no longer sees a valid invalidation path.\nEncode each part of the path instead and then join it back together\n\nThat keeps the  characters in place while encoding the apostrophe and any other special characters in the filename.\n","excerpt":"This issue is <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>. That creates a query string and encodes the leading / so cloudfront no longer sees a valid invalidation path. &hellip; ","title":"How to invalidate CloudFront path if the filename contains an apostrophe?"},{"tags":["javascript","typescript","amazon-web-services","amazon-cloudfront"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79983514,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1784740509,"creation_date":1784634089,"body":"Using the JavaScript SDK V3 for CloudFront, I need to invalidate a path that contains the &#39; character. If that makes a difference, the CF distribution is caching an S3 bucket, and the &#39; character is valid and supported in S3 filenames.\nI have tried encoding the file&#39;s path using , using , both of which leave the  character unchanged, or using  as suggested in this answer to How do you pass an apostrophe through a URL?, which encodes it as .\nThe code for doing that looks like this:\n\nHowever, regardless of how I try to encode it, I get the following error:\n\nI don&#39;t want to disallow using the apostrophe in filenames unless it&#39;s the only possible solution. There isn&#39;t anything in the AWS documentation that suggests this character is impossible to use, or that it is somehow different from other special characters (most of which work fine when using URI encoding in my experience).\n","excerpt":"I have tried encoding the file&#39;s path using encodeURI, using encodeURIComponent, both of which leave the &#39; character unchanged, or using new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>({ text: &quot;something&#39;s wrong&quot; }).toString() as &hellip; Subfolder/Filename of some kind.json&quot; */\nexport async function invalidateCacheForFile(filePath: string) {\n    const cloudfrontClient = new CloudFrontClient(config);\n\n    const pathEncoded = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; ","title":"How to invalidate CloudFront path if the filename contains an apostrophe?"},{"tags":["javascript","jquery","css","html"],"question_score":5,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79970228,"is_answered":false,"question_id":51095254,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1782725749,"creation_date":1782725749,"body":"You can&#39;t show an alert after  in the same request. Once the form is submitted, the browser leaves the current page and the JavaScript context is destroyed.\nIf you need to display a success message after the page reloads, pass a flag (for example, via a query string) and check for it when the page loads:\n\nThen redirect to something like:\n\nAlso note that showing a success message in  only means the client-side validation passed. It does not mean the server successfully processed the form.\n","excerpt":"need to display a success message after the page reloads, pass a flag (for example, via a query string) and check for it when the page loads:\nwindow.onload = function () {\n    const params = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; ","title":"Success message after form submission"},{"tags":["twilio","twilio-api"],"question_score":6,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79857095,"item_type":"question","score":6,"last_activity_date":1782205409,"creation_date":1767041808,"body":"I am attempting to implement the WhatsApp typing indicator using the documentation provided at https://www.twilio.com/docs/whatsapp/api/typing-indicators-resource.\nHowever, I have not yet had a successful API call.\nThe request fails with the following error:\n\ninvalid or incomplete inbound data in MDR\n\nDoes anyone know which parameters, besides the  and the channel , I should be passing?\nHere is the code I am currently using:\n\n","excerpt":"_MasterAccountAuthToken}`)\n  .toString(&#39;base64&#39;);\n        \nconst params = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>();\nparams.append(&#39;messageId&#39;, messageId);\nparams.append(&#39;channel&#39;, &#39;whatsapp&#39;);\n        \nawait axios.post( &hellip; ","title":"Twilio WhatsApp Typing Indicator"},{"tags":["javascript","jquery","arrays","json"],"question_score":-1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":78539357,"item_type":"question","score":-1,"last_activity_date":1780123004,"creation_date":1716816546,"body":"I&#39;m trying to convert JSON data into a QR code, so when a user scans it, the JSON data should be displayed. I&#39;ve been using the jquery.qrcode.min.js library for this. Here&#39;s an example of the JSON data format:\n{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;4444455551223&quot;,&quot;tableid&quot;:&quot;2222221111111444&quot;,&quot;areacode&quot;:&quot;A0010&quot;,&quot;catid&quot;:&quot;QWAAD&quot;}\np.s id,tableid,areacode,catid these are fetched dynamically from url parameter\ne.g www.demo.com?id=4444455551223&amp;tableid=2222221111111444&amp;areacode=A0010&amp;catid=QWAAD\nHowever, the issue I&#39;m facing is that when I scan the generated QR code with a mobile device, it only shows &quot;4444455551223&quot; as the result, rather than displaying the entire JSON data. Any ideas on how to solve this?\nCode used to generate QR code.\n\n","excerpt":"jQuery(&#39;#output&#39;).qrcode( {  \n\n\n            const queryString = window.location.search;\n      \n            const urlParams = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>(queryString);\n\n            const id = urlParams.get(&#39;id&#39; &hellip; ","title":"Error on converting Json data to QR code using JS library"},{"tags":["javascript","jquery","arrays","json"],"question_score":-1,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":78540407,"is_answered":false,"question_id":78539357,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1780123004,"creation_date":1716830929,"body":"I don&#39;t know how you got your code to give you even one value in the QR code. Your code as is, was not runnable at all for me.\nThere were two major problems:\n\nYour const and var definitions need to be moved out of the .qrcode() function body.\nSetting your myJSON was all wrong. You did not end up with any of your data in it.\n\nHere is the edited code that worked for me:\n\n","excerpt":"the edited code that worked for me:\n// NOTE Your const a var definitions need to be moved out of the .qrcode() function body\n\nconst queryString = window.location.search;\n\nconst urlParams = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; ","title":"Error on converting Json data to QR code using JS library"},{"tags":["cookies","iframe","safari","cross-domain","banno-digital-toolkit"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":79947769,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1779979487,"creation_date":1779955929,"body":"We are finding that in Safari on Desktop that our dashboard plug-in card including other plug-in cards we didn&#39;t create such as Zogo are not loading properly and just displaying the error invalid_request.\nBased on my observation, the main difference between Chrome and Safari is that Safari doesn&#39;t complete the OIDC flow.  For example, when the plug-in card iframe via the src attribute makes the initial request to our backend (which is the first redirect URI defined for the external application), we return a direct to a URL such as https://digital.garden-fi.com/a/consumer/api/v0/oidc/auth for the digital garden with all the necessary query string parameters.  Then when the browser follows that request it gets a redirect to a URL such as https://digital.garden-fi.com/login?returnUrl=/a/consumer/api/v0/oidc/interaction/3KVqUfoZ_Dumc2Lrv0ABC/login-complete.  In Chrome it actually proceeds with the OIDC flow and works.  From what I can tell, the iframe doesn&#39;t have access to the Digital Garden cookies (maybe the  ConsumerJWT cookie) to tell it that the user is in a signed in state and so it treats the user as needing to sign-in to the Digital Garden again.\nI am fairly certain that this was working in the recent past so I expect either a Banno or Safari update has broken this type of integration recently.  This problem isn&#39;t just isolated to the Digital Garden as it is happening across the different instances of Banno used by our clients.\nI tried turning off the following settings in Safari but it didn&#39;t make a difference:\n\nPrevent cross-site tracking\n\nOpt-in partitioned cookies (CHIPS)\n\nDisable Full 3rd-Party Cookie Blocking (ITP)\n\n\nI also enabled &quot;Intelligent Tracking Prevention debug mode&quot; and didn&#39;t see it log to the console anything that it might have blocked.\nHere is a minimal, reproducible example:\n:\n\n:\n\nSteps to reproduce:\n\nSign into https://jackhenry.dev/portal/\n\nUnder &quot;Build external applications&quot; copy the &quot;Client ID&quot; and &quot;Client secret&quot; and paste in  in lines 20 and 21 respectively (see TODOs in CONFIG object).\n\nSet the first Primary redirect URI to &quot;https://localhost:8080/auth&quot; without quotes and second as &quot;https://localhost:8080/auth/callback&quot; without quotes and hit Save.\n\n\n\nUnder &quot;Plugin configuration&quot;:\n\nSet Title to &quot;My Test Plugin&quot; without quotes\n\nSet Description to &quot;Test Description&quot; without quotes\n\nSet Initial Height to 300\n\nHit Save\n\n\n\nInstall Node.js if not already installed\n\nPlace the  and  files in a directory\n\nIn a terminal navigate to that directory and run the following commands:\n\n\nConfigure your OS to trust  and restart Safari\n\nSign into https://digital.garden-fi.com/login via Safari\n\nNotice that the plug-in card says &quot;invalid_request&quot; and then try with Chrome and see it displays &quot;Hello World!&quot;\n\n\n\n","excerpt":"createPkcePair();\n    const state = createState();\n    stateStore.set(state, codeVerifier);\n\n    const authBaseURL = `${CONFIG.api.environment}/a/consumer/api/v0/oidc/auth`;\n    const params = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; ","title":"Safari getting invalid_request in plug-in cards"},{"tags":["javascript","file","processing","p5.js"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":78032001,"is_answered":false,"question_id":36478149,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1779430110,"creation_date":1708497620,"body":"Numerous approaches for data persistence exist. Deciding which approach to take depends on your needs. Begin by clarifying what data you need to persist and the scope of data access (single user or all users). Another consideration is complexity, particularly for learners who are new to coding.\nFor simple use cases, like persisting a user&#39;s preferences, or building a per-user high scores table, I&#39;d start with . It&#39;s simple to use and well-supported. The disadvantage is that it&#39;s specific to a single user (although that may be all you need). If the user clears their local browser files, local storage will be cleared.\nThe API is simple:\n\nFor more complex data, like an array or object, you can use  and serialize the data structure going into local storage and  to restore it after pulling it out.\nIf you want to use a backend server so you can share data across clients, SQLite is a good starter database, but this is still pretty complex for most beginners since it involves SQL. Avoiding SQL can be done using an object relational mapper (ORM) like Sequelize, but such libraries also tend to have a steep learning curve.\nA JSON file is not scalable for serious apps, but should be far more accessible to learners and is a great entry point to backend development. I&#39;ll add an example below you can run and modify to meet your needs.\nHere&#39;s the server.js code, which uses a single library called Express, which makes it easier to develop a server application:\n\nand create a scores.json data file with an empty object in it:\n\nHere&#39;s the p5.js script (public/index.html):\n\nInstall Node on your system and open a terminal, then run\n\nWith the server running, navigate to http://localhost:5000 to run the client app and click the button to add a few scores. Scores should persist even after cycling the server on and off, and you&#39;ll see the scores.json file updating.\nI&#39;ve tried to keep the code as simple as possible, but there&#39;s still a lot going on here. Try making adjustments and researching the individual concepts in isolation, like JSON file reading and writing, creating routes in Express, and so on. Adjust the app to work with a simple game of your own creation.\nIn the interest of keeping things compact, I&#39;ve avoided most error handling. For complex data, a POST route is preferable to a GET route as I used here, but it&#39;s a little bit more complex to implement.\nYou can run the server locally, or you can code and host the server on a free tier on a hosting site like Render, although there may be restrictions, like slow startup time. Always-on server apps usually cost money.\nYou don&#39;t have to use Node.js for the backend, although it&#39;s probably the easiest option for those already using JS on the frontend. Hosting on a site like Python Anywhere and developing your code using Python and Flask may be appealing to those who have experience with it.\nFirebase or Supabase are other persistence options to consider.\nKeep in mind, p5 is just a rendering library for HTML5, so anything that works for browser JS will work for p5 as well.\n","excerpt":"String.fromCharCode(floor(random(97, 122)));\n  }\n\n  return res;\n}\n\nfunction renderScores() {\n  scoresEl.elt.textContent = JSON.stringify(scores, null, 2);\n}\n\nfunction addScore() {\n  const params = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; ","title":"P5Js modifying local file such as json or xml or table"},{"tags":["reactjs","react-hooks","onclick"],"question_score":-1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":75533257,"item_type":"question","score":-1,"last_activity_date":1779373379,"creation_date":1677071949,"body":"I&#39;m working on an React app that lists my favorite music albums. Everything worked fine until i implemented the change order (ascend, descend) and the sort list buttons.\nOn the first click it doesn&#39;t react, but on the second click it logs the value of the previous click and renders the previous value.\nI&#39;m fetching the data from server database, and everything works when I manually change order and sort values and than save the changes. It renders immediately and it looks good, but when i implemented the function to change those values on a button click, then the problems started.\nI&#39;ve tried multiple solutions that I found on other similar questions, but nothing worked.\nI&#39;ve tried useCallback, I&#39;ve added useEffect for console.log, I&#39;ve tried using ordinary variables instead of setState, changing the dependencies in useEffect, and several other solutions I found but nothing worked for me. It always changes on the second click, and then on every other click it shows the previous value.\nThis is my code:\n\nI understood that React should re-render every time I use setState, but I don&#39;t understand why I&#39;s not changing in my situation. Any help is very appreciated.\n","excerpt":"${new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>(sort).toString()}`).then(response =&gt; {\n            response.json().then(albums =&gt; {\n                setAlbums(albums);\n            });\n        });\n    }, [albums]);\n\n    function &hellip; ","title":"useState and onClick issue in React"},{"tags":["reactjs","react-hooks","onclick"],"question_score":-1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":75533320,"is_answered":false,"question_id":75533257,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1779373379,"creation_date":1677072255,"body":"It looks like your useEffect hook dependencies are incorrect:\n\nSince the hook has  as a dependency, then when the data loads, the hook will be triggered again. This will cause a loop, and re-render the page each time.\nRemove albums from your dependency list, add  and , and this will fix your problem.\n\nThe sort needs to stringified, as the hook will not pick up changes in a complex object natively.\n","excerpt":"${new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>(sort).toString()}`).then(response =&gt; {\n            response.json().then(albums =&gt; {\n                setAlbums(albums);\n            });\n        });\n    }, [albums]);\n\nSince the hook &hellip; ${new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>(sort).toString()}`).then(response =&gt; {\n            response.json().then(albums =&gt; {\n                setAlbums(albums);\n            });\n        });\n    }, [JSON.stringify(sort), key &hellip; ","title":"useState and onClick issue in React"},{"tags":["node.js","express","oauth","passport.js"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79941837,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1778996356,"creation_date":1778858370,"body":"I&#39;ve been attempting to develop an application which has access to OnShape&#39;s API, using OAuth as the authentication method. However, I&#39;ve been running into an issue both with my own code and with the examples they&#39;ve provided. The original redirect correctly steers the user to the OnShape sign in page, which happens correctly, but after you sign in, you get a 400 Invalid request error. As an exercise after I first started having this issue, I was able to successfully implement OAuth for my google account and access information via their API, but I can&#39;t seem to find a way around this issue- so I don&#39;t know if an issue specific to OnShape, my understanding of OAuth or their process, etc.\n\nHere are the screenshots from my browser.\nBrowser - onshape oauth sign in\nonshape oauth sign in\n400 Error\nhttps://oauth.onshape.com/oauth/authorize?response_type=code&amp;redirect_uri=https:%2F%2Flocalhost:443%2Fauth%2Fonshape%2Fcallback&amp;scope=OAuth2ReadPII&amp;client_id=REDACTED\nThe example they show using express and passport for authentication. Here is the link to the instructions for https://onshape-public.github.io/docs/auth/oauth/\nFor the sake of this question, let&#39;s assume that my client id and client secret are correct. I&#39;m 99% sure I&#39;ve provided the callback correctly and registered things correctly with OnShape... but maybe there&#39;s an issue there.\nThe OnShape urls seem to align with what they have in documentation (the two callback URLS are just because I&#39;ve tried just running it on local host and using nginx as a https server, but the same issue persists)\n\nPassport strategy borrows from &quot;passport-onshape&quot;, but I did try constructing the redirect url myself as a debugging exercise and this seemed to yield the same error (although i guess its possible for other reasons). Strategy initialization and my auth routes are shown below.\nbase routes from index.js\n\nconfig/auth.js\n\nroutes/auth.js\n\nPlease let me know if anything else would be helpful to provide. This is my first time doing anything with OAuth, and the solution so far has been elusive.\n","excerpt":"protection; in production, you should store this in the session and verify it in the callback\n        scope: &#39;OAuth2ReadPII&#39;,\n    }\n));\n\nrouter.get(&#39;/debug-auth&#39;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const params = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; ","title":"passport-onshape OAuth authorization flow fails with 400 &quot;Invalid request&quot; after authentication"},{"tags":["stripe-payments"],"question_score":8,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":73867291,"item_type":"question","score":8,"last_activity_date":1778269421,"creation_date":1664280429,"body":"I have one problem in integration Stripe into my React application. I use code from official Stripe documentation. It works expected. My question is how to check is payment succeeded without using return_url ? Am I required to use return url ? I found in Stripe documentation redirect: &quot;if_required&quot; option, but that doesnt make anything. I just get error problem in my console if I put this object in confirmPayment method. I would like have scenario is payment successfull that client navigate to some Confirmation page and to get message payment successfully.\nApp.jsx\n\nCheckoutForm.jsx\n\n","excerpt":"stripe) {\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const clientSecret = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>(window.location.search).get(\n      &quot;payment_intent_client_secret&quot;\n    );\n\n    if (! &hellip; ","title":"How display payment succeeded without return_url param in Stripe"},{"tags":["javascript","html","ecmascript-6"],"question_score":29,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79932100,"is_answered":false,"question_id":42406520,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1777219578,"creation_date":1777217655,"body":"Since I&#39;ve been working on this issue for the past few days, I tried implementing Sphinxxx&#39;s solution.\nUnfortunately, in the   function, ‘checkbox’ fields aren&#39;t exported if their value isn&#39;t ‘checked’. See Sphinxxx&#39;s warnings !\nSo I modified the code as follows:\n\nKind regards to all,\nMartin\n","excerpt":"e.name}=${e.value}`);\n                    break;\n            }\n        });\n        return result.join(&quot;&amp;&quot;);\n    }\n\n    deserialize_form(form, serialized_form_data) {\n        const entries = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; ","title":"Populate an HTML Form from a FormData object"},{"tags":["javascript","regex"],"question_score":-1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79925091,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79925071,"item_type":"answer","score":5,"last_activity_date":1776274466,"creation_date":1776135295,"body":"\n\nWhy are the  and  substrings not matched as the splitter part, and therefore fall to the array?\n\n\nThey are inside capture groups, which are included in the resulting array. Reference\n\nIf  is a regex, the returned  also contains the captured groups for each separator match; see below for details.\n\n\n\n\nWhy are there two empty substrings at the array beginning?\n\n\nExample Same as above, the first split to match on is &#39;^abc=&#39;, the left side is empty string, the first capture is &#39;^&#39;, so empty string, the second capture is &#39;=&#39;, and then the right half of that split is &#39;def&#39;.\n\n\n\nHow can I get the expected output?\n\n\n\nWithout modifying the regex, just skip those known indices.\n\n\nMake the regex groups non-capturing and skip the match before &quot;^&quot;.\n\n\nIf you truly can make all the assumptions you have already made, perform the split in a multi step\n\n\nInstead of trying to split, just a straight regex match with positive look behind/aheads (regexr.com does warn about lack of support in some browsers, but seems to work fine in the majors)\n\n\nUse URLSearchParams\nNot sure if this is related to what you&#39;re really trying to do, but there is a built in for working with query strings.\n\n\n\n","excerpt":"=&amp;|$)/g)\n\n\nUse <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>\nNot sure if this is related to what you&#39;re really trying to do, but there is a built in for working with query strings.\nvar params = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>(&quot;abc=def&amp;ghi=jkl&amp; &hellip; ","title":"JavaScript regular expression behaviour"},{"tags":["google-chrome-extension","vite"],"question_score":3,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":73896403,"item_type":"question","score":3,"last_activity_date":1775727857,"creation_date":1664458225,"body":"Basically what I need is generate one single file that contain all vendors and dependencies for each entry..\n\nIn this case.. will be one file for popup, background and content_script\nHere is one example of ContentScript.tsx file...\n\nWith this configuration I&#39;m getting this..\n\nand my content_scripts/index.js ..\n\nAs you can see.. I don&#39;t want this  statement ..\n","excerpt":"appContainer.firstChild);\n\n  const root = createRoot(rootElement, {});\n  root.render(\n    &lt;React.StrictMode&gt;\n      &lt;Badge query={query} /&gt;\n    &lt;/React.StrictMode&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nconst searchParameters = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; ","title":"Generate one single file for each entry with Vite"},{"tags":["next.js","i18next","react-i18next"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79921564,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79920981,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1775567463,"creation_date":1775567463,"body":"The only way I found to do it is to redirect to a temporary  URL using &#39;s  section, and then redirect one more time to a clean URL without the  param in the component. It&#39;s similar to before but at least there&#39;s no blank page flickering anymore, it&#39;s the content flickering, which is a little better.\n\nthen in the component,\n\n","excerpt":". */\n\nthen in the component,\nconst pathname = usePathname();\n\nuseEffect(() =&gt; {\n    const searchParams = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>(window.location.search);\n    let langParam = searchParams.get(&quot;lang&quot;);\n    if &hellip; ","title":"Next.js automatic redirect of legacy language-enabled URL (&quot;...-es&quot;) to core page with automatic i18next translation"},{"tags":["instagram-api","instagram-graph-api"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79912715,"item_type":"question","score":1,"last_activity_date":1774262834,"creation_date":1774262834,"body":"I am building a web application using React (frontend deployed on Vercel) and Node.js/TypeScript (backend deployed on Vercel) that integrates with the Instagram Business API using the 2024 Meta Instagram Platform API (without Facebook Page requirement).\nI am following the standard OAuth 2.0 authorization code flow:\n\nFrontend redirects user to Instagram OAuth\n\nInstagram redirects back to my callback page with an authorization code\n\nFrontend sends the code to my backend\n\nBackend exchanges the code for an access token\n\n\nThe problem is Step 4 always fails with this error:\n\nFrontend (React + Vite) deployed on Vercel:\n\n\nBackend (Node.js + TypeScript) deployed on Vercel:\n\nWhat I Have Verified redirect_uri matches exactly in all places:\n| Instagram App Dashboard | `https://dmflow-seven.vercel.app/instagram/callback` | | Frontend OAuth URL | `https://dmflow-seven.vercel.app/instagram/callback` | | Backend token exchange | `https://dmflow-seven.vercel.app/instagram/callback` |\n","excerpt":"};\n\n  handleCallback();\n}, [navigate]);\n\nBackend (Node.js + TypeScript) deployed on Vercel:\nconst connect = async (userId: string, code: string, redirectUri: string) =&gt; {  const params = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; ","title":"Instagram API OAuth Code &quot;Error validating verification code.Please make sure your redirect_uri is identical&quot; even though redirect_uri matches exactly"},{"tags":["angular","http"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":3,"is_answered":false,"question_id":45588018,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1773302930,"creation_date":1502274310,"body":"I need to send username and password to a login api using angular http post request. Here is my code:\n\n\n\nI am getting the following error in response:\n\n\n  Request URL:http://111.22.333.44:55/api/token\n  Request Method:OPTIONS\n  Status Code:400 Bad Request\n  Remote Address:172.31.131.13:85\n  Referrer Policy:no-referrer-when-downgrade\n\n\nAm I missing something?\n","excerpt":"Here is my code:\n\nconst params = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>();\n    params.set(&#39;username&#39;, username);\n    params.set(&#39;password&#39;, password);\n    this.headers = new Headers({ &#39;content-type&#39;: &#39;application/x-www-form-urlencoded &hellip; ","title":"How do I pass user name and password in an http post request from angular 4"},{"tags":["ios","google-chrome","oauth-2.0","chrome-ios"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79905452,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1773237936,"creation_date":1773237936,"body":"I&#39;m implementing Google OAuth in a Node.js/Express app. Standard sign-in (selecting an already-listed Google account) works correctly. But when a user taps &quot;Use another account&quot; on the Google account chooser in Chrome for iOS, the flow never completes — the new account is never added and the user is never authenticated. Instead, the &quot;google.com wants to sign in&quot; popup appears again, and the process repeats indefinitely.\nThe user is stuck: they cannot add a new account and cannot sign in.\nBackground: the iOS Chrome workaround\nChrome for iOS cannot handle OAuth redirects in its own process. When the user taps &quot;Sign in with Google&quot;, iOS intercepts the Google redirect and opens it in a sandboxed SFSafariViewController (a separate WebKit process with an isolated cookie jar). The OAuth callback lands in Safari/WebKit, not Chrome.\nTo bridge this, I implemented a one-time login token flow:\n\nClient (iOS Chrome): intercepts the sign-in button click, fetches a Google OAuth URL from the server as JSON, then navigates its own tab to Google with \n\nServer callback (received by SFSafariViewController/WebKit): authenticates the user, creates a short-lived token, redirects to \n\nChrome tab (now at ): consumes the token and establishes its own session\n\n\nThis works for standard sign-in. It fails when the user picks &quot;Use another account.&quot;\n\nCode\nClient-side: sign-in button click handler (iOS Chrome only)\n\nServer: returns the Google OAuth URL as JSON (for iOS Chrome)\n\nServer: OAuth callback — creates login token for iOS Chrome\n\nServer: exchange-token endpoint — Chrome consumes the token\n\nToken store (MongoDB, 60s TTL)\n\n","excerpt":"window.location.href = btn.href; });\n  });\n})();\n\nServer: returns the Google OAuth URL as JSON (for iOS Chrome)\napp.get(&#39;/auth/mentor-register/google-url&#39;, authLimiter, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const params = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; ","title":"Chrome for iOS: Google OAuth &quot;use another account&quot; flow loops back to sign-in instead of completing authentication"},{"tags":["next.js","datatables"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79895971,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1772556769,"creation_date":1772006408,"body":"I&#39;m using  in a Next.js project. I have exactly 12 columns defined. I noticed that every time my URL parameters change, the table triggers many identical AJAX requests instead of just one.\nThe Problem: I am using  (where  is ) to force the table to update when the URL changes. Without this , the table does not detect the URL change and doesn&#39;t refresh the data. However, with the , it seems React destroys and remounts the component\nI tried removing  and using  instead, but I get the same result. When I use DataTables and change a filter (e.g., the brand filter), it sends 8 HTTP requests for that single change. If I change the filter again, it sends 16 HTTP requests, then 24, and so on.\nHow can I avoid this behavior and ensure only one request is sent per filter change? Thanks for your help!&quot;\n\n\n\nwith reference\n\nwith paramsKey\n\nhere my package.json\n\n","excerpt":"tableRef.current.dt().ajax.reload();\n    }\n  }, [searchParams]);\n\n  return (\n    &lt;DataTable\n      ref={tableRef} \n      ajax={async (data: any, callback) =&gt; {\n        try {\n          const params = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; window.location.search : &#39;&#39;;\n\n  return (\n    &lt;DataTable\n    key={paramsKey} \n      ajax={async (data: any, callback) =&gt; {\n        try {\n          const params = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>(window.location.search &hellip; ","title":"DataTables serverSide triggers multiple duplicate AJAX requests in nextjs"},{"tags":["google-places-autocomplete"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79887075,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1772107756,"creation_date":1770775721,"body":"I want users to be able to type addresses into the input field I supply and all I need for output is the latitude and longitude for the address, which is just from one city. I don&#39;t need to place it on a map. I&#39;m using vanilla JS - i.e. no frameworks.\nThere appear to be several available options in the documentation, some of which have given me &quot;function not defined&quot; errors; and others saying &quot;New&quot; version (generally giving no output, or errors) or &quot;legacy&quot; cases. There&#39;s also a widget and also a recommendation to use the &quot;Place&quot; version.\nThe only script I could get to do some of what I need is the following:\n\nIf I apply the location restriction, some addresses aren&#39;t found, without any restriction, they are.\n(1) Is this a &quot;latest&quot; version? If not, how should I modify it?\n(2) How do I modify this so it uses my own input field (as opposed to the one generated by the script?\n(3) Is this the approach likely to be least costly?\n(4) Should I be using some other approach entirely?\n","excerpt":"need is the following:\n(g=&gt;{var h,a,k,p=&quot;The Google Maps JavaScript API&quot;,c=&quot;google&quot;,l=&quot;importLibrary&quot;,q=&quot;__ib__&quot;,m=document,b=window;b=b[c]||(b[c]={});var d=b.maps||(b.maps={}),r=new Set,e=new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; ","title":"Autocomplete without generated input field and no map"},{"tags":["typescript","next.js","calendar"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":2,"is_answered":true,"question_id":79896487,"item_type":"question","score":2,"last_activity_date":1772050278,"creation_date":1772047037,"body":"I’ve integrated Microsoft Calendar into my website so users can manage multiple calendars through WhatsApp.\nIt works well with Google Calendar.\nMicrosoft Calendar also works, but after some time it shows an “authorization expired” error.\nHere is my integration source code.\n\nCould you please help me fix this issue?\n","excerpt":": string): string {\n    const params = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>({\n      client_id: MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CONFIG.clientId,\n      response_type: &quot;code&quot;,\n      redirect_uri: redirectUri,\n      scope: MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CONFIG.scopes.join &hellip; ","title":"Microsoft Calendar Authorization Expiration Issue"},{"tags":["typescript","next.js","calendar"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79896508,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79896487,"item_type":"answer","score":2,"last_activity_date":1772050278,"creation_date":1772050278,"body":"\nHere is full solution.\n","excerpt":": string): string {\n    const params = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>({\n      client_id: MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CONFIG.clientId,\n      response_type: &quot;code&quot;,\n      redirect_uri: redirectUri,\n      scope: MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CONFIG.scopes.join &hellip; OAuthTokens&gt; {\n    try {\n      // Use direct OAuth2 token endpoint instead of MSAL\n      // This gives us the actual refresh_token string (MSAL hides it in its internal cache)\n      const body = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; ","title":"Microsoft Calendar Authorization Expiration Issue"},{"tags":["websocket","puppeteer","n8n"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79894958,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79891548,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1771880535,"creation_date":1771880535,"body":"My guess is that your proxy credentials include special characters that are failing because they aren’t being URL encoded properly, or that using both individual query parameters and the JSON launch parameter is causing a conflict.\nTry using the following snippet to generate the connection URL and connect via Puppeteer:\n\nLinks to relevant documentation:\n\nThird Party Proxies\n\nLaunch Options\n\n\n","excerpt":"http://USERNAME:PASSWORD@p.webshare.io:80&quot;);\n\nconst launchOptions = {\n  headless: false,\n  stealth: true,\n  blockConsentModals: true,\n  externalProxyServer: proxyUrl\n};\n\nconst queryParams = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; ","title":"Hi, I’m using Puppeteer connected to a self-hosted Browserless instance via WebSocket, but externalProxyServer does not seem to be applied"},{"tags":["mailgun","cloudflare-pages","mailgun-api"],"question_score":-1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79894523,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79672554,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1771843606,"creation_date":1771843606,"body":"Thanks for the heads up was quite tricky to figure out using the Cloudflare fetch (not the same as browser fetch) here&#39;s my code that worked (the  is my own utility wrapper for ). Hope it helps someone\n\n","excerpt":"from &quot;@/util&quot;\n\ntype MessageData = {\n  to: string[]\n  subject: string\n  text: string\n}\n\nexport const sendMail = async (data: MessageData) =&gt; {\n  const env = await getEnv()\n\n  const formData = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; ","title":"Mailgun sending API works on local dev server, but catches Bad Request error on Cloudflare Pages deployment?"}],"has_more":true,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":235}